1
00:00:00,646 --> 00:00:04,701
So how can we make sure that we can clear

2
00:00:04,927 --> 00:00:06,590
these list items, that we can make these

3
00:00:06,687 --> 00:00:08,222
remove buttons here work?

4
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:09,957
Well,

5
00:00:10,685 --> 00:00:11,946
back in our code here,

6
00:00:12,642 --> 00:00:15,360
we can simply go to these buttons

7
00:00:16,428 --> 00:00:21,430
and then add the hx-delete attribute on that

8
00:00:21,462 --> 00:00:21,850
button.

9
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,597
And that will then send a delete request

10
00:00:25,836 --> 00:00:28,323
HTTP request whenever that button is

11
00:00:28,483 --> 00:00:28,820
clicked.

12
00:00:29,623 --> 00:00:31,725
Now, just to make sure that we're all on the

13
00:00:31,725 --> 00:00:32,399
same page,

14
00:00:32,993 --> 00:00:37,502
DELETE requests are standard HTTP requests

15
00:00:37,710 --> 00:00:40,759
just like GET or POST requests. They're just

16
00:00:40,759 --> 00:00:42,749
using a different HTTP verb.

17
00:00:44,225 --> 00:00:47,306
Now, just by sending a DELETE request alone,

18
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:50,483
you're not going to delete anything though.

19
00:00:51,125 --> 00:00:53,949
It's instead the backend code you write that

20
00:00:54,399 --> 00:00:56,613
in the end decides what's going to happen.

21
00:00:57,288 --> 00:00:59,617
When you send a POST request, you also don't

22
00:00:59,713 --> 00:01:02,846
magically add data somewhere. Instead, you

23
00:01:02,846 --> 00:01:05,577
just decide to handle a POST request and you

24
00:01:05,577 --> 00:01:08,164
then write the code that controls what

25
00:01:08,212 --> 00:01:10,605
happens upon such a POST request.

26
00:01:12,067 --> 00:01:14,092
And it's the same now with a DELETE request.

27
00:01:14,397 --> 00:01:18,188
We can send a DELETE request or we can get

28
00:01:18,413 --> 00:01:22,285
HTMX to send one by adding the hx-delete

29
00:01:22,349 --> 00:01:25,402
attribute on an element. And therefore HTMX

30
00:01:25,402 --> 00:01:27,748
will send such a request whenever the

31
00:01:28,390 --> 00:01:29,322
most obvious

32
00:01:29,852 --> 00:01:32,713
event for an element occurs, which in case

33
00:01:32,713 --> 00:01:33,870
of a button is a click event.

34
00:01:35,126 --> 00:01:37,493
But we then have to decide to which path

35
00:01:37,799 --> 00:01:40,182
that request should be sent and we then have

36
00:01:40,214 --> 00:01:42,903
to write the code on the backend to handle

37
00:01:43,032 --> 00:01:45,583
that incoming request and to then actually

38
00:01:45,841 --> 00:01:46,680
delete something.

39
00:01:48,279 --> 00:01:51,265
Now let's start with the path. Here, I guess

40
00:01:51,265 --> 00:01:53,218
it makes sense to again send this to /goals.

41
00:01:54,362 --> 00:01:56,433
But then I actually also

42
00:01:57,076 --> 00:01:58,618
wanna incorporate the

43
00:01:59,244 --> 00:02:03,339
ID or in this case here, the index of the

44
00:02:03,355 --> 00:02:05,090
element that should be deleted

45
00:02:05,829 --> 00:02:07,692
into this path here.

46
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:11,273
Therefore, I'll actually adjust this string

47
00:02:11,386 --> 00:02:15,160
here to dynamically inject the index so that

48
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:16,493
the DELETE request is sent to

49
00:02:17,071 --> 00:02:20,781
/goals/0 for the first element in the list,

50
00:02:21,573 --> 00:02:24,393
/1 for the second element and so on.

51
00:02:26,210 --> 00:02:28,414
And that of course now means that we need to

52
00:02:28,478 --> 00:02:31,325
add an appropriate route here in this

53
00:02:31,325 --> 00:02:32,966
Express demo application.

54
00:02:34,222 --> 00:02:37,039
And there we can set up a route for DELETE

55
00:02:37,039 --> 00:02:39,422
requests by using the DELETE method on this

56
00:02:39,679 --> 00:02:40,516
app object

57
00:02:41,370 --> 00:02:42,883
and by then defining the path

58
00:02:43,463 --> 00:02:45,781
for which we wanna listen to DELETE

59
00:02:45,781 --> 00:02:46,294
requests.

60
00:02:47,274 --> 00:02:49,490
And in my case here, that's /goals

61
00:02:50,149 --> 00:02:53,938
and then this dynamic segment, which is 0

62
00:02:54,098 --> 00:02:56,089
for the first element, 1 for the second

63
00:02:56,170 --> 00:02:57,197
element, and so on.

64
00:02:58,257 --> 00:03:00,778
And when using Express, that can be

65
00:03:00,826 --> 00:03:04,102
represented by using a colon and then any

66
00:03:04,214 --> 00:03:06,526
identifier of your choice, like for example,

67
00:03:06,639 --> 00:03:08,421
idx for index.

68
00:03:09,673 --> 00:03:12,307
This simply tells Express that this will be

69
00:03:12,307 --> 00:03:14,571
a dynamic segment which is not known in

70
00:03:14,571 --> 00:03:15,004
advance,

71
00:03:15,534 --> 00:03:17,976
but which we will be able to extract for

72
00:03:18,169 --> 00:03:19,394
every incoming request.

73
00:03:21,103 --> 00:03:23,037
So therefore here we have that request

74
00:03:23,086 --> 00:03:25,600
handler and we can now get the index by

75
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:26,793
accessing req.params.idx.

76
00:03:31,089 --> 00:03:34,455
This is how we extract the actual concrete

77
00:03:34,650 --> 00:03:38,178
index that was submitted in that path as

78
00:03:38,356 --> 00:03:39,748
part of the incoming request.

79
00:03:40,703 --> 00:03:44,087
And I'm accessing.idx here because I have

80
00:03:44,184 --> 00:03:47,698
chosen idx as an identifier after the colon

81
00:03:47,943 --> 00:03:49,234
here in this route path.

82
00:03:51,410 --> 00:03:53,761
So that then gives me the index of the item

83
00:03:53,777 --> 00:03:54,808
that should be deleted

84
00:03:55,452 --> 00:03:57,289
and we can then therefore go ahead

85
00:03:57,885 --> 00:04:00,542
and access the courseGoals and then use the

86
00:04:00,542 --> 00:04:04,091
splice method, which is a standard array

87
00:04:04,091 --> 00:04:05,414
method in JavaScript

88
00:04:05,946 --> 00:04:08,398
to remove an element from the array.

89
00:04:09,753 --> 00:04:12,009
And for that, we just need to pass the index

90
00:04:12,075 --> 00:04:14,241
of the element that should be removed to

91
00:04:14,289 --> 00:04:17,184
splice and then a 1 thereafter as a second

92
00:04:17,233 --> 00:04:19,724
argument to make it clear that it's just

93
00:04:19,724 --> 00:04:21,875
this one element that should be removed.

94
00:04:23,482 --> 00:04:25,580
That will therefore then remove it from the

95
00:04:25,580 --> 00:04:25,951
list

96
00:04:26,855 --> 00:04:28,485
and then what?

97
00:04:29,114 --> 00:04:30,986
How do we proceed here?

98
00:04:32,802 --> 00:04:35,793
Well, then as a next step, we wanna make

99
00:04:35,857 --> 00:04:38,816
sure that this list item is also removed

100
00:04:38,816 --> 00:04:41,935
here from that rendered list of goals.

101
00:04:44,443 --> 00:04:46,917
And that is something that's fairly easy to

102
00:04:46,934 --> 00:04:50,184
achieve with HTMX. Because if you're

103
00:04:50,249 --> 00:04:52,772
handling a DELETE request and you then wanna

104
00:04:52,772 --> 00:04:56,281
make sure that the element or some element

105
00:04:56,699 --> 00:04:59,736
in the DOM that was responsible for sending

106
00:04:59,768 --> 00:05:01,664
the request is deleted,

107
00:05:02,242 --> 00:05:02,999
all you have to do

108
00:05:03,622 --> 00:05:06,228
is send back an empty response.

109
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:09,545
So a response with an empty body.

110
00:05:11,566 --> 00:05:15,518
If you do that, HTMX will take that response

111
00:05:15,534 --> 00:05:16,938
content, which is

112
00:05:17,535 --> 00:05:17,890
empty,

113
00:05:18,745 --> 00:05:21,747
and it will then do what it always does with

114
00:05:21,812 --> 00:05:22,443
responses.

115
00:05:23,025 --> 00:05:25,760
It will render it into the element that

116
00:05:25,873 --> 00:05:28,785
triggered that request. So in this case, it

117
00:05:28,785 --> 00:05:30,566
would render it into this button.

118
00:05:31,688 --> 00:05:33,932
It would essentially delete this text here,

119
00:05:34,224 --> 00:05:34,680
remove,

120
00:05:35,265 --> 00:05:36,484
and replace it with the empty response.

121
00:05:37,818 --> 00:05:40,110
So with nothing, so we would have an empty

122
00:05:40,142 --> 00:05:40,513
button.

123
00:05:41,433 --> 00:05:43,048
That's of course not what I want here

124
00:05:43,048 --> 00:05:43,274
though.

125
00:05:44,030 --> 00:05:46,096
Therefore, what I'll do instead

126
00:05:46,628 --> 00:05:50,616
is I will add the hx-target element

127
00:05:51,633 --> 00:05:54,827
and target the entire list item here so that

128
00:05:54,891 --> 00:05:57,938
the content of that list item is replaced

129
00:05:57,938 --> 00:05:58,632
with nothing

130
00:05:59,293 --> 00:06:01,542
and it's therefore essentially deleted.

131
00:06:03,292 --> 00:06:07,175
Now, the CSS selector for targeting this

132
00:06:07,528 --> 00:06:10,357
list item here can be the ID of that list

133
00:06:10,357 --> 00:06:10,598
item.

134
00:06:11,579 --> 00:06:14,086
So therefore here I can use #goal-

135
00:06:15,259 --> 00:06:18,064
and then of course dynamically construct

136
00:06:18,096 --> 00:06:20,970
that ID by again using the index, which I am

137
00:06:21,084 --> 00:06:22,756
using for setting the ID as well.

138
00:06:24,450 --> 00:06:27,553
So with that, I'm then targeting this entire

139
00:06:27,553 --> 00:06:28,167
list item.

140
00:06:29,897 --> 00:06:32,095
Now out of the box, as you learned,

141
00:06:32,646 --> 00:06:36,577
HTMLX would then swap the content inside of

142
00:06:36,593 --> 00:06:39,867
that list item with the response body. So it

143
00:06:39,867 --> 00:06:42,530
would delete the content inside the list

144
00:06:42,595 --> 00:06:44,857
item in this case here since the response

145
00:06:44,857 --> 00:06:45,900
body will be empty.

146
00:06:46,622 --> 00:06:48,468
But that's not exactly what I want here.

147
00:06:48,708 --> 00:06:51,356
Instead, I wanna replace the entire element

148
00:06:51,420 --> 00:06:52,688
and therefore delete it.

149
00:06:53,907 --> 00:06:57,117
Hence, we should also set hx-swap here to

150
00:06:57,357 --> 00:07:00,791
outerHTML so that the entire list item is

151
00:07:00,888 --> 00:07:03,214
replaced with the response body, so with

152
00:07:03,311 --> 00:07:04,255
that HTML. Empty body

153
00:07:05,024 --> 00:07:07,153
and therefore the entire list item will be

154
00:07:07,153 --> 00:07:07,612
deleted.

155
00:07:10,100 --> 00:07:12,274
Now of course, we should add these same

156
00:07:12,451 --> 00:07:13,144
attributes

157
00:07:13,756 --> 00:07:16,623
down here where I'm also rendering such list

158
00:07:16,719 --> 00:07:18,813
items. And of course repeating it like this

159
00:07:18,813 --> 00:07:21,144
is not ideal, but for the moment we'll do it

160
00:07:21,144 --> 00:07:21,465
like this.

161
00:07:22,027 --> 00:07:24,823
I will soon show you an alternative approach

162
00:07:24,935 --> 00:07:27,763
though, but for the moment this is how we'll

163
00:07:27,827 --> 00:07:28,132
do it.

164
00:07:28,936 --> 00:07:31,437
Of course down here though, I don't have the

165
00:07:31,518 --> 00:07:34,649
index variable, instead I calculated the

166
00:07:34,649 --> 00:07:36,990
index like this. And since I now need this

167
00:07:36,990 --> 00:07:39,573
in multiple places, I will for convenience

168
00:07:39,573 --> 00:07:42,156
create that index variable and store the

169
00:07:42,156 --> 00:07:42,995
result of this

170
00:07:43,525 --> 00:07:45,793
calculation, if you wanna call it like this,

171
00:07:46,324 --> 00:07:46,597
in it.

172
00:07:47,224 --> 00:07:49,894
And then use index here and in those other

173
00:07:49,942 --> 00:07:50,328
places.

174
00:07:52,967 --> 00:07:55,374
With that, if we save that and we reload,

175
00:07:56,441 --> 00:07:58,896
if I enter Learn HTMX, that's added.

176
00:07:59,452 --> 00:08:02,034
If I learn advanced concepts,

177
00:08:02,744 --> 00:08:03,454
that's added.

178
00:08:04,276 --> 00:08:05,712
And now if I click on

179
00:08:06,470 --> 00:08:07,987
learn advanced concepts,

180
00:08:08,810 --> 00:08:10,504
that is removed as you see.

181
00:08:11,133 --> 00:08:13,667
And the same happens with learn HTMX.

182
00:08:15,813 --> 00:08:19,538
However, we'll face a problem here if we do

183
00:08:19,554 --> 00:08:20,993
it the other way around.

184
00:08:23,431 --> 00:08:26,454
If I now start by deleting learn HTMX,

185
00:08:26,996 --> 00:08:29,676
and I then click Learn Advanced Concepts, it

186
00:08:29,804 --> 00:08:32,677
looks like it worked, but if I reload,

187
00:08:33,334 --> 00:08:34,875
the advanced concepts are back.

188
00:08:35,597 --> 00:08:37,988
They weren't actually deleted even though it

189
00:08:38,004 --> 00:08:39,288
looked like this on the UI.

190
00:08:40,315 --> 00:08:41,984
But it only looked like that because of

191
00:08:42,016 --> 00:08:45,209
course we always return this empty response

192
00:08:45,225 --> 00:08:47,375
and therefore we always in the end delete

193
00:08:47,375 --> 00:08:49,076
the element that sent the request,

194
00:08:49,798 --> 00:08:51,997
but we didn't actually delete it here.

195
00:08:52,767 --> 00:08:53,617
And why is

196
00:08:54,195 --> 00:08:56,217
that and how can we solve it?
